<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=bimaristan">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bimaristan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">805</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as bimarestan, maristan, dar al-shifa, darüşşifa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A bimaristan is a hospital of the medieval Islamic world; the first true one was probably founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> under the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>, and the first documented general hospital arose there in 805. Funded by charitable endowments (waqf), bimaristans admitted patients regardless of religion or gender, kept separate wards and written patient records, and trained physicians in lecture halls and on the wards. Later foundations included the Al-Nuri Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> (1156) and the Al-Mansuri Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> (1284), which could hold 8,000 beds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bimaristan.jpg" alt="Bimaristan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bimaristan_Argun_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>805</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Baghdad</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> · <a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Al-Walid I’s foundation at Damascus in 707 may have been a leprosarium rather than a hospital, and Harun al-Rashid’s traditional hospital of 787 is questioned.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galenic medicine<span>162 CE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/the-canon-of-medicine">The Canon of Medicine<span>1025</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=bimaristan">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
